course: dictionary
lesson: 1158
title: Bring and what it can do
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word bring is small, but it can mean several things. Its meaning depends on the words around it, so read the sentence first. «Bring your book to school» means carry your book to the place where you will be.
explain: Bring can also mean to come with someone. «She brought her little brother along to the park» means she came to the park with him. Bring about means to make something happen, and bring up a child means to care for it as it grows.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «One kind act, like bringing soup to someone ill, can end a feud.» Carrying soup to a sick person fits the sentence, so bringing means carrying here. A feud is a long quarrel between people or families.
fact: Bring means to carry something to a place where someone is.
fact: To bring someone is to come with them, as in «brought her little brother along».
fact: Bring about means to make something happen, and bring up a child means to care for it as it grows.
fact: Bring is a verb, so it changes: bring, brings, brought, bringing.
fact: Phrases such as bring back, bring in, bring together and bring along each change the meaning a little, so check the words around them.
exercise: «Bring your book to school.» What does bring mean here? | To lose something on the way | Take something away from school | *To carry something to a place where someone is | To buy something new
exercise: «She brought her little brother along to the park.» What does brought along mean? | She forgot his name | *She came to the park with him | She left him at home | She threw him a ball
exercise: «Cats bring prey home.» What is being brought? | The owner's car | *Prey, such as a mouse or a bird | The bell on the collar | The house
test: «Mum will bring up the children with love.» What does bring up mean? | To lift the children into the air | To teach the children to swim | *To care for children as they grow | To carry the children to school
test: «Many people are bringing back their languages.» What does bringing back suggest? | Forgetting a language quickly | Speaking very loudly | *Keeping alive something that had been lost | Throwing away something old
exam: «The new road will bring about big changes in the village.» What does bring about mean? | To forget the village | *To make big changes happen | To carry the village away | To visit the village
